    "markdown": "# Quantum Physics\n\n  [Submitted on 24 Jul 2010 (this version), \n\n    *latest version 26 Sep 2011*(v2)]\n# Title:EPR, Bell, and Quantum Locality\n\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\n            Abstract:Maudlin has claimed, Am. J. Phys. 78 (2010) p. 121, that no local theory can reproduce the predictions of standard quantum mechanics that violate Bell's inequality in the case of Bohm's version (two spin-half particles in a singlet state) of the Einstein-Podolsky-Rosen problem. It is argued that, on the contrary, standard quantum mechanics itself is a counterexample to Maudlin's claim, as it is local in the appropriate sense (measurements here do not influence what is going on over there) when formulated using consistent principles in place of the inconsistent appeals to \"measurement\" found in current textbooks. This sheds some light on the claim of Blaylock (ibid. p. 111) that counterfactual definiteness is an essential ingredient in derivations of Bell's inequality.\n    \n\n## Submission history\n\nFrom: Robert B. Griffiths [view email]\n**[v1]**Sat, 24 Jul 2010 19:20:25 UTC (30 KB)\n\n**[v2]**Mon, 26 Sep 2011 21:56:24 UTC (48 KB)\n\n### References & Citations\n\n    \n    Loading...\n\n# Bibliographic and Citation Tools\n\n            Bibliographic Explorer \n\n        *(What is the Explorer?)*\n            Connected Papers \n\n        *(What is Connected Papers?)*\n            Litmaps \n\n        *(What is Litmaps?)*\n            scite Smart Citations \n\n        *(What are Smart Citations?)*\n# Code, Data and Media Associated with this Article\n\n            alphaXiv \n\n        *(What is alphaXiv?)*\n            CatalyzeX Code Finder for Papers \n\n        *(What is CatalyzeX?)*\n            DagsHub \n\n        *(What is DagsHub?)*\n            Gotit.pub \n\n        *(What is GotitPub?)*\n            Hugging Face \n\n        *(What is Huggingface?)*\n            ScienceCast \n\n        *(What is ScienceCast?)*\n# Demos\n\n# Recommenders and Search Tools\n\n              Influence Flower \n\n          *(What are Influence Flowers?)*\n              CORE Recommender \n\n          *(What is CORE?)*\n# arXivLabs: experimental projects with community collaborators\n\narXivLabs is a framework that allows collaborators to develop and share new arXiv features directly on our website.\n\nBoth individuals and organizations that work with arXivLabs have embraced and accepted our values of openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\n\nHave an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? **Learn more about arXivLabs**.",
